# REGRESSION — `on error:` is an ALTERNATIVE path, not a straight-line scope.
#
# Handlers run ONLY when the function exits via an error and never on normal
# return (docs/language-reference.md S10.7; the lowering pushes the body to
# `on_error_blocks` rather than lowering it in sequence,
# src/ir/lowering/stmts/mod.rs:457-461).
#
# The liveness walker's `walk_block` applies KILLS as well as uses, so walking
# a handler straight into `live` lets a kill INSIDE the handler delete a name
# that is still live on the NORMAL path — an under-approximation, the
# dangerous direction. The handler must be UNIONED the way an `if` branch is.
#
# This shape regressed at `c5860a68` — the commit that made `walk_stmt`
# exhaustive lumped `OnError` in with `Unsafe`/`NamedScope` under a comment
# claiming "the block runs in sequence with its neighbours", which is false.
# Measured at c5860a68: ICE rc 101 `local _1 read after MoveZero` on C and LLVM.
#
# Found by R45 Track D brief-review pass 14, one pass after the commit that
# introduced it — which is what the confirming pass is FOR.
#
# ⚠ The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector. Without it the moved-from read still finds live memory.
# Probe: `on error:` body is NOT straight-line (it runs ONLY on error paths),
# but walk_stmt lumps it with Unsafe/NamedScope as a straight-line scope.
# A KILL inside the handler therefore leaks onto the NORMAL path.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f() throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    on error:
        s = mk("c", "d")
    print(s)
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f()! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
